        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Ignore the calendar and use the water test. A few drops in four or five places, twice a year: beading means the sealer is working, spreading and darkening means that spot is due. Wear is never even, so the strip beside the sink and the zone in front of the hob will fail years before a quiet corner does.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then match the response to the system. A penetrating sealer is cleaned, dried and topped up in an hour with no visible edge. A film sealer lasts far longer and then needs assessing honestly for whether it takes a recoat or a strip. And if you take one new habit from this page, take the sacrificial wax — ten minutes every few months that roughly doubles how long everything underneath lasts.
          </p>
        </div>

